<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="b48f9206">&lt;p&gt;
    The application architecture illustrates the structure, relationships, constraints, rules, and operations. It
    portrays different architecture tiers such as presentation, business logic and business services, security, third-party
    communication and indicates the necessary components in each tier. The model should clarify the role (underlying
    business function or process) that each of these components plays in the context of the overall system. It
    should reflect the results of process alignment and portfolio rationalization.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The application architecture helps to define and document:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The structure of the system 
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The recurring interactions and dependencies between sets of components 
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The components present within an enterprise, each of which may be made up of smaller components
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Application architectures can be documented at three levels:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Conceptual level&lt;/strong&gt; - focuses on specifying the high-level components that are required to implement
        the solution. These specifications are typically box diagrams that portray a conceptual view of the system
        components/services that are technology and product neutral.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Logical level&lt;/strong&gt; - focuses on specifying the components&apos; responsibilities and characteristics
        necessary to deliver the requirements. At this level it is appropriate to show component integration and
        communication views. These specifications can be technology and/or product neutral.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Physical level&lt;/strong&gt; - focuses on how to implement the components to meet the previously established
        specifications.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;br /&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="8d7e9ff4"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="75b77b73">When this artifact is used to document the current state, it may be created in a simplified form.</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="690cb241"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="f4c077f2">&lt;p&gt;
    The purpose of this artifact is to:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Illustrate how the identified application components and interfaces fit together to communicate an
        application-centric view of the business functions/processes to the stakeholders, as well as to guide the overall
        design and development of the solution.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Address the stakeholder concerns related to application.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Help identify candidate architecture roadmap components based upon gaps between the baseline and target
        architectures.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="impactOfNotHaving" nodeId="3fd74c16">If this artifact is not produced for the current state it will not be possible to accurately evaluate the current
environment and it is likely that key details may be overlooked when designing the new solution.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
If this artifact is not produced for the target solution it will be very difficult to produce a consistent design and there
is a higher chance of rework being required due to a lack of overall application architecture.&lt;br /&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="reasonsForNotNeeding" nodeId="8226a886">If the solution contains no or very little automation this artifact may not be required. In some cases, this artifact may
already exist for the current systems so it may only need to be validated and not created.</attribute>
<attribute name="briefOutline" nodeId="2d3cb427">&lt;p&gt;
    The application architecture consists of:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Introduction:&lt;/strong&gt; Describes the context, purpose, scope, and intended audience of the application
        architecture.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Notation:&lt;/strong&gt; If appropriate, describes any notation used in the document.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Overview of Layers and Components&lt;/strong&gt;. Describes different architecture tiers and systems/components
        in each tier.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Component Descriptions&lt;/strong&gt;: Describes in detail each component&apos;s responsibilities and interfaces,
        required service levels, risk, design rationale, and implementation approach.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Application Landscape Diagram&lt;/strong&gt;: Identifies and documents applications and components that are used
        by multiple lines of business across an organization. This technique is commonly leveraged by practitioners for the
        current-state application assessment.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Application Component Diagram&lt;/strong&gt;: Describes components, interfaces, services, and relationships in a
        business functional context. It is common to leverage a multi-tier architecture technique to portray this
        view. 
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Application Component Metrics&lt;/strong&gt;: Identifies and documents application/system metrics such as total
        number of users per application, total number of transactions processed per day, etc. This technique can be
        leveraged for both current-state application assessment and target-state application development.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Integration Components:&lt;/strong&gt; These include components such as APIs, Runtime clients and
        Controllers, etc. that play a crucial role with establishing proper communication within the application
        architecture landscape. It may include components to support application monitoring.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Integration Patterns:&lt;/strong&gt; These include patterns such as Service Oriented Architecture (SOA),
        Enterprise Application Integration (EAI), etc. to adopt a standardized and leading industry-based integration
        approach that an application architecture solution can benefit from.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="representationOptions" nodeId="467d8004">&lt;p&gt;
    The model is typically represented in a graphical form and application modeling techniques such as Unified Modeling
    Language (UML), Building Block notation, Archimate, or simple Box and Line diagrams are leveraged to develop these
    models. Also consider what tools are available at a client.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
